# SplatBake — Licensing

Copyright (c) 2026 Blender-Claude

This project is split into two halves with two different licenses. The split
is along a real line in the code, not a formality.

    splatbake/            GPL-3.0-or-later   the Blender add-on
    splatbake/splatcore/  Apache-2.0         Blender-free data core

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later AND Apache-2.0


## The add-on — GPL-3.0-or-later

Every module outside `splatcore/` calls Blender's Python API (`bpy`, `gpu`,
`mathutils`, `bpy_extras`). Blender's own license statement holds that the
Python API is an integral part of Blender and that published scripts using
it must be shared under a GPL-compliant license; the Blender Extensions
platform requires `SPDX:GPL-3.0-or-later` specifically, whatever license the
code carried originally. So this half is GPL-3.0-or-later, and the add-on
as a distributed whole is governed by it. Full text: `LICENSE-GPL-3.0.txt`.


## The core — Apache-2.0

`splatcore/` contains no Blender code at all: no `bpy`, no `gpu`, no
`mathutils`. It parses `.ply`, `.splat` and SOG captures, evaluates
spherical harmonics, and provides the spatial grid — plain Python and numpy
that would run identically in a web viewer, a converter, or a render farm.
Nothing about it is derived from Blender, so nothing requires it to be
copyleft. It is offered under Apache-2.0, patent grant included. Full text:
`splatcore/LICENSE`.

The one place Blender used to leak in was SOG image decoding. That is now a
hook (`splatcore.sog.IMAGE_READER`): the add-on installs Blender's decoder
at registration, while a standalone user gets Pillow automatically. Taking
an argument instead of an import is what keeps the boundary real.

Using it on its own:

    from splatcore import loaders
    data = loaders.load_any("capture.ply", want_sh=True)

**Do not add an `import bpy` to that package.** Doing so would make it a
Blender derivative and collapse the separation this file describes.


## Why they can ship together

Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible with GPL-3.0: Apache code may be
incorporated into a GPLv3 work, and the result is governed by the GPL. That
is exactly the arrangement here — the add-on is the GPLv3 work, and the
Apache core is a component of it. The reverse does not hold, so GPL code
must never be added to `splatcore/`.


## Authorship

The project is maintained at https://github.com/Blender-Claude, and the
copyright notices name that account. Copyright vests in the human author
behind it; a handle is a perfectly valid way to hold it, since copyright law
recognises pseudonymous works.

Parts of this project were written with the assistance of Claude, an AI
assistant made by Anthropic, credited as a development tool in `NOTICE` and
in the README. That credit is deliberately not a claim of ownership. An AI
cannot hold copyright: the US Copyright Office requires human authorship,
instructs applicants not to name the AI technology or its provider as an
author, and the courts have upheld that position — the Supreme Court
declined to revisit it in March 2026. Work made with AI assistance stays
protectable to the extent a human directed and shaped it, which is the case
here.

If anyone else has contributed copyrightable code, their permission is
needed for the license terms above, whether or not they are named. Not
listing a contributor does not remove their rights in what they wrote.


## Not legal advice

This file records the copyright holder's intent and summarises publicly
stated policy. It is not legal advice. If licensing matters commercially
here — a client contract, a company policy on copyleft, paid distribution —
have a lawyer review it.
